Magnesium-Rich Foods for a Balanced Diet

Magnesium is a mineral that plays a role in normal muscle and nerve function and many other processes in the body. Eating a varied diet can help you obtain magnesium from everyday foods.

Nuts and seeds are convenient sources. Almonds, peanuts, cashews, pumpkin seeds, and sunflower seeds can be added to breakfast bowls, salads, yoghurt, or snacks.

Leafy green vegetables can also contribute magnesium. Spinach, amaranth, and other greens can be prepared as curries, stir-fries, dals, or soups.

Whole grains are another useful food group. Oats, brown rice, whole-wheat chapati, and other whole grains can be incorporated into everyday meals.

Legumes such as lentils, chickpeas, beans, and peas provide magnesium along with protein and fibre. They are versatile enough to be used in curries, salads, soups, wraps, and snacks.

Bananas can contribute some magnesium as well and are easy to include in breakfast or snacks. You can slice one over oatmeal, add it to yoghurt, or simply eat it on its own.

Dark chocolate contains magnesium too, so a small portion can fit into a balanced diet if you enjoy it. There is no need to completely avoid chocolate during your period.

Instead of searching for one “magic” food, try building meals that include several food groups. For breakfast, oatmeal with banana and almonds is one example. Lunch could be dal, rice, and spinach. A snack could be yoghurt with fruit and seeds.

A varied diet is generally more useful than relying on supplements without professional advice. If you have specific nutritional concerns, a qualified healthcare professional can help determine what you need.

Period symptoms vary from person to person. Food may support overall nutrition, but it cannot guarantee relief from severe pain or other medical symptoms. If menstrual symptoms regularly interfere with school, work, sleep, or everyday life, consider discussing them with a healthcare professional.

